    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-header-center">
        <p class="section-eyebrow">Quick Answers</p>
        <h2 id="faq-title" class="section-heading">Frequently asked questions about federal benefits</h2>
        <p class="section-subtext">Concise, verified answers to the most common questions seniors and families ask.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-grid">
        <div class="faq-card">
          <h3 class="faq-question">What is the Social Security COLA for 2027?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">
            The Social Security Cost-of-Living Adjustment (COLA) for 2027 is 3.6%, officially announced by the Social Security Administration on October 14, 2026. The increase takes effect with January 2027 payments (SSI on December 31, 2026). The maximum taxable earnings wage base rises to $192,300. Use our <a href="/tools/social-security-cola-calculator/" style="color: #0A3D3A; text-decoration: underline;">Social Security COLA 2027 Calculator</a> to see your new monthly check, or read our in-depth <a href="/social-security/social-security-cola-2027/" style="color: #0A3D3A; text-decoration: underline;">Social Security COLA 2027 Guide</a>.
          </p>
          <a href="/tools/social-security-cola-calculator/" class="faq-link">Calculate your 2027 COLA increase →</a>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-card">
          <h3 class="faq-question">What is my Social Security Full Retirement Age?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">
            Your Full Retirement Age (FRA) depends on your birth year. For anyone born in 1960 or later, FRA is 67. For those born 1943–1954, it is 66. For birth years 1955–1959, FRA increases by two months per year from 66 to 67.
          </p>
          <a href="/tools/social-security-full-retirement-age-calculator/" class="faq-link">Check your exact FRA →</a>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-card">
          <h3 class="faq-question">How much is the Medicare Part B late enrollment penalty?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">
            The Part B penalty is 10% of the standard premium ($202.90 in 2026) for each full 12-month period you were eligible but not enrolled. This surcharge is permanent and added to your monthly premium for life.
          </p>
          <a href="/tools/medicare-late-enrollment-penalty-calculator/" class="faq-link">Calculate your penalty amount →</a>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-card">
          <h3 class="faq-question">What should I do if I receive an IRS CP2000 notice?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">
            A CP2000 proposes changes to your taxes based on third-party reporting. Do not pay automatically. Review the discrepancies, verify documents, and respond in writing using the Response form included with your CP2000 notice, by the due date printed on it (usually 30 days).
          </p>
          <a href="/tools/cp2000-response-letter-sample/" class="faq-link">Use the dispute letter tool →</a>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-card">
          <h3 class="faq-question">When do I have to start taking Required Minimum Distributions (RMDs)?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">
            Under the SECURE 2.0 Act, you must start taking RMDs at age 73 if born between 1951 and 1959, or age 75 if born in 1960 or later. RMDs apply to traditional IRAs and 401(k)s, but not Roth IRAs during your lifetime.
          </p>
